# MalloySim scenario: a stack of three boxes standing on the floor, in 2D (M40).
#
# The 2D sibling of box_stack.scn. Until M40 a box contact manifold (a box on
# a ground plane, M37; box against box, M36) was reduced to ONE contact at its
# centroid, and one contact cannot stop two corners at once: a stack's boxes
# kept a phantom downward velocity while the positional correction pushed them
# back, and a staggered stack crept sideways until it fell. This one, on the
# solver before M40: its top box drifted 0.055 m in the first 10 s and the
# stack fell at 26.1 s.
#
# M40 resolves every point of every manifold (issue #73), with accumulated
# impulses clamped to push only and to the Coulomb bound, iterated until the
# contact residual is below 1e-8 v_scale (v_scale is the larger of |g| dt and
# the fastest contact body's |v| + |omega| x its size) or 100 sweeps, warm
# started from the last step. The contacts are solved BEFORE the positions
# move, and a nonlinear Gauss-Seidel pass then removes any depth beyond a slop
# of 1e-6 x the pair's size (the smaller box's larger half-width) without
# touching a velocity.
#
# Three boxes of different sizes and masses, each offset by 0.08 to the right
# of the one below, placed exactly touching and at rest. They must stand. The
# 2D checks read whole-system quantities (energy, kinetic energy, momentum,
# angular momentum), so standing is checked through them: a box that sinks
# lowers the potential energy, one that creeps carries momentum, one that
# rocks carries angular momentum and kinetic energy.

type rigid

dt 0.002
steps 5000
output_every 500

gravity 0.0 -9.8
restitution 0.0
friction 0.5
ground 0.0 1.0 0.0

# rigid_box <mass> <hx hy> <px py> <angle> <vx vy> <omega>
#
# Bottom, middle, top. The bottom box's top edge is at y = 0.5, the middle
# box's bottom; the middle box's top edge is at y = 0.9, the top box's bottom.
rigid_box 1.2  0.50 0.25   0.00 0.25   0.0   0.0 0.0   0.0
rigid_box 0.9  0.45 0.20   0.08 0.70   0.0   0.0 0.0   0.0
rigid_box 0.6  0.40 0.15   0.16 1.05   0.0   0.0 0.0   0.0

# Expected, from a real run of the shipped binary:
#
#   step      0   E   1.52880000e+01   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00  0.00000000e+00
#   step    500   E   1.52880000e+01   L   2.61123982e-12   p  -7.99993414e-12 -2.41605682e-12
#   step   5000   E   1.52880000e+01   L   2.47709929e-11   p  -6.68043178e-12 -3.57467265e-11
#
# E holds at its starting value to the eight digits printed for the whole run.
# L and p are the solver's residual, 1e-10 at most at any report: rounding
# that will differ between compilers, which is why the checks below bound them
# rather than pin them. The top box stays within 2e-10 of where it was put.
#
# The invariants and where each tolerance comes from. The rest phase stops at
# a residual r = 1e-8 |g| dt = 1.96e-10, and a box resting on a support at that
# residual moves at most v_rest = 3 r (1 + 2 R / w) and turns at most
# omega_rest = 2 sqrt(3) r / w, for R its corner distance and w its smaller
# width: v_rest is 2.26e-9, 2.04e-9 and 1.90e-9 for the top, middle and bottom
# box, all below 2.3e-9, and omega_rest 2.26e-9 at most. Box k up rests on k
# interfaces, so it moves at most k times one interface's allowance.
#
# 1. The energy starts as the potential sum m g y = 9.8 (1.2 x 0.25 +
#    0.9 x 0.70 + 0.6 x 1.05) = 15.288, all of it.
#
# 2. At the end it is the same less what the sag allows. Each interface sits
#    within the slop, 1e-6 x 0.5, 1e-6 x 0.45 and 1e-6 x 0.40 from the bottom
#    up, plus 4 v_rest dt (1.9e-11); the cap's share, 0.75^100 of a step's
#    gravity, is below 1e-16. Box k up sinks by the slops of the k interfaces
#    below it: 9.8 (1.2 x 5e-7 + 0.9 x 9.5e-7 + 0.6 x 1.35e-6) = 2.22e-5, plus
#    the kinetic bound; 2.3e-5.
#
# 3. It is at rest: kinetic energy at most n^2 = 9 times the sum of each box's
#    rest bound (1/2 m v_rest^2 + 1/2 I omega_rest^2), 5.3e-17, rounded up to
#    1e-16; momentum at most sum m k 2.3e-9 = 4.8 x 2.3e-9 = 1.1e-8, rounded
#    up to 1.2e-8; angular momentum about the origin at most
#    sum (I k omega_rest + m |r| k 2.3e-9) = 0.38 x 2.26e-9 + 3.48 x 2.3e-9 =
#    8.9e-9, rounded up to 9e-9.
#
# A stack that falls changes the energy by joules; one that creeps or rocks
# leaves momentum, angular momentum and kinetic energy far above these bounds.
# (On the solver before M40 the angular momentum at step 5000 was -2.6e-2.)
#
# On the ASCII view (an orthographic view of the plane): three points, the
# boxes' centres, stepping up and to the right, that never move.

# Machine-checkable assertions, evaluated against a real run by the scenario
# tests. `step 0` is the state before any step, and the tolerance is absolute.
# The rigid `energy` quantity adds the gravitational potential.
# check step 0    energy      1.52880000e+01   tol 1e-12
# check step 0    kinetic     0.00000000e+00   tol 0
# check step 5000 energy      1.52880000e+01   tol 2.3e-5
# check step 5000 kinetic     0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-16
# check step 5000 momentum_x  0.00000000e+00   tol 1.2e-8
# check step 5000 momentum_y  0.00000000e+00   tol 1.2e-8
# check step 5000 angular     0.00000000e+00   tol 9e-9
